::STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What is a joint frequency in a two-way table?

{
=A. A count inside the table for one row category and one column category#Correct. A joint frequency combines categories from both variables.
~B. A row total only#A row total is a marginal frequency.
~C. A column total only#A column total is a marginal frequency.
~D. The title of the table#The title describes the table but is not a frequency.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
What is a marginal frequency?

{
~A. A count inside the table#That is a joint frequency.
=B. A row total or column total#Correct. Marginal frequencies appear at the margins of the table.
~C. A category label#Labels name groups but are not frequencies.
~D. A causal conclusion#Frequency tables do not automatically prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A row has counts 12 and 18. What is the row total?

{
~A. 6#This subtracts the counts.
~B. 18#This is one cell, not the total.
=C. 30#Correct. 12 + 18 = 30.
~D. 216#This multiplies the counts.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
A column has counts 9 and 21. What is the column total?

{
~A. 12#This subtracts the counts.
~B. 21#This is one cell, not the total.
~C. 189#This multiplies the counts.
=D. 30#Correct. 9 + 21 = 30.
}

::STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.7
Question:
Online students choose video notes much more often than in-person students. What may this suggest?

{
=A. Learning format and note preference may be associated#Correct. Different distributions across groups suggest possible association.
~B. Learning format caused note preference#A two-way table alone does not prove causation.
~C. There is no relationship because both are categories#Categorical variables can be associated.
~D. The table has no joint frequencies#A two-way table contains joint frequencies.
}
